# The translation setup is perhaps easiest to think about starting from
# the English page. www/index.html is the source of all user-visible
# text and the PO catalogues in this directory are translations of
# strings extracted from that page. We don't author a second HTML tree
# for translated pages.
#
# There are a few bits of information needed to make this work. The ITS
# rules describe which parts of the HTML contain translatable text.
# LINGUAS says which translations we publish. POTFILES says which HTML
# pages participate in extraction. And, finally, each language named in
# LINGUAS has a matching PO catalogue here.
#
# The resulting HTML pages belong to the website, so they are built
# under www/ in the output tree. See www/buildfile for that side of the
# arrangement. In particular, it makes the catalogue for each translated
# page a direct prerequisite of that page. This means changing fr.po,
# for example, rebuilds the French page without making all the other
# translations look out of date.
#
# There is one gettext detail that ends up affecting the HTML itself.
# msgfmt --xml replaces element text but doesn't replace attribute
# values. Thus any text that needs a translation has to exist as element
# text. This is the reason an icon-only link in www/index.html has a
# visually hidden span instead of putting its label exclusively in
# aria-label.
#
# The page description is the one current exception. It lives in the
# content attribute of <meta name="description"> and so remains English
# in translated pages.

# Start by turning LINGUAS into the actual catalogue targets.
#
# Note that these are source targets. Listing a language in LINGUAS
# means that we expect its catalogue to already exist in this directory.
# In particular, there is no rule here that invents an empty catalogue
# for a newly listed language. A missing catalogue should fail the build
# since the LINGUAS entry would otherwise claim that we ship a
# translation which we don't have.
#
catalogues =
for l: $linguas
  catalogues += po{$l}

# POTFILES serves two related purposes. xgettext reads it to find the
# input pages and build2 needs the same pages as prerequisites of the
# POT target. Derive one from the other here so there is a single
# definition of the input set.
#
# Ignore comments in POTFILES (if any) and keep the remaining lines as
# paths relative to the project root. The regular expression
# deliberately does no further interpretation. POTFILES is already the
# gettext-facing description of this set and we want to pass the same
# names to xgettext below.
#
potfiles = $process.run_regex(cat $src_base/POTFILES, '^([^#].*)$', '\1')

pages =
for f: $potfiles
{
  # A POTFILES entry names the source file. Split that into its
  # directory and extension-less leaf so we can spell the same file as a
  # build2 html{} target.
  #
  d = $path.directory($f)
  n = $path.base($path.leaf($f))

  # Resolve the target from the project source root. This is the target
  # that actually supplies the English text, so changing it must make
  # the template out of date.
  #
  pages += html{$src_root/$d$n}
}

# Generate the message template from the English pages.
#
# We intentionally don't keep iw4x.pot under version control. It
# contains text derived entirely from the files named by POTFILES, so
# keeping a checked-in copy would give us two representations of the
# same information. Regenerating it makes the HTML the authority and
# leaves the PO catalogues as the authored translation state.
#
# There is a small path detail here that is easy to miss. xgettext
# normally records the path by which it reached each source file in the
# "#:" reference. Run it with the project source root as its directory
# and feed it the relative paths from POTFILES. This gives us references
# such as www/index.html:42 independent of where somebody happened to
# check out.
#
pot{iw4x}: $its $pages
{{
  # xgettext is part of the rule implementation even though its
  # executable is not a normal file prerequisite. Record its version in
  # the dependency database so changing the tool version makes this
  # recipe out of date. This is useful here since the generated POT file
  # is partly a product of xgettext's formatting and extraction
  # behaviour.
  #
  depdb string "$xgettext_version"

  # Keep the normal build output at the operation level. The complete
  # command is still available through build2's usual higher verbosity
  # levels.
  #
  diag xgettext $>

  # $<[0] is the ITS prerequisite. Tell xgettext to use those extraction
  # rules for the HTML, then change its working source directory
  # logically to $src_root so that POTFILES can stay project-relative.
  #
  # Keep --files-from pointed at the source POTFILES file instead of
  # expanding $pages back into command-line arguments. This means
  # xgettext and the prerequisite construction above consume the same
  # file directly.
  #
  # --no-wrap is intentional. PO files are source material and stable
  # paragraph-shaped msgids make changes much easier to review than
  # wrapping that moves when a nearby word changes.
  #
  $xgettext "--its=$path($<[0])"                \
            "--directory=$src_root"             \
            "--files-from=$src_base/POTFILES"   \
            --from-code=UTF-8                   \
            --add-comments                      \
            --no-wrap                           \
            --package-name="$project"           \
            --package-version="$version"        \
            --msgid-bugs-address=wroy@proton.me \
            "--output=$path($>)"
}}

# Updating an existing catalogue is a source-tree maintenance operation.
#
# A normal build reads the PO files and must leave them alone. msgmerge
# --update does the opposite: it edits each catalogue to match the newly
# extracted template. Keep that operation behind an explicit alias so
# running a normal build can never rewrite translator-authored files.
#
# The intended maintainer invocation is:
#
#   b po/update-po
#
# Building this alias first brings pot{iw4x} up to date and then merges
# that template into every catalogue named by LINGUAS.
#
alias{update-po}: pot{iw4x} $catalogues
{{
  diag msgmerge $>

  for c: $catalogues
  {
    # The first prerequisite is pot{iw4x}. Merge it into this catalogue
    # in place and ask msgmerge not to leave the usual ~ backup next to
    # the source file. Version control already gives us the useful
    # history here.
    #
    # Keep the previous msgid on fuzzy matches. A fuzzy entry often
    # represents an English sentence that was edited, and retaining the
    # old msgid gives the translator the context needed to update the
    # translation.
    #
    # As with xgettext above, don't let msgmerge reflow entries.
    #
    $msgmerge --update      \
              --backup=none \
              --previous    \
              --no-wrap     \
              $path($c) $path($<[0])
  }
}}

# Make the generated template part of the default build.
#
# It isn't needed as an installed website file. Building it here is
# still useful since extraction is part of validating the authored page.
#
# A broken ITS rule or HTML input should fail an ordinary build at the
# point where the translation source is extracted instead of first
# appearing when somebody explicitly updates the catalogues.
#
# Keep the authored catalogues and translation configuration in the
# default target as well. This gives build2 the complete
# translation-side dependency graph from the directory root.
#
./: pot{iw4x} $catalogues $its $loc file{LINGUAS POTFILES}
